  "summary": "A monumental towered gateway entrance to Hindu temple complexes, particularly in Dravidian architecture.",
  "description": "Characterized by its towering, pyramid-like structure with elaborate sculpted figures of deities, mythical creatures, and intricate geometric patterns. These imposing gateways served as both architectural entrances and symbolic representations of the cosmic mountain Meru, marking the transition between the secular and sacred realms.",
  "culturalContext": "The gopuram — Dravidian temple's monumental entrance tower — grew from Chola modesty to Nayak-era giants: Madurai's Meenakshi southern tower (51m, 1,500+ stucco figures), Srirangam's 13-tier Rajagopuram (72m). Built as stepped pyramids of divine population — deities, guardians, mythic processions — the gopuram anchors temple-city axial planning, is re-carved and repainted in cycles, and defines the skyline grammar of Tamil Nadu, Kerala and the diaspora (Singapore, London) alike.",
